## The rights basis differs by state — read the relevant state's own README, not just this file

This is not one blanket claim. Each state's source documents come from a different kind of
publisher with a different rights basis, checked and stated honestly per state rather than
assumed to be uniform:

| State | Document type | Basis for redistribution | Detail |
|---|---|---|---|
| Washington | State agency (DSHS) court-monitor reports | Fair use — DSHS asserts no copyright notice on these, but WA has no public-domain-by-default policy either; not a public-domain claim | `wa/README.md` |
| Oregon | 15 court-monitor / neutral-expert reports (privately authored by Dr. Pinals, OHA only hosts them) | Fair use (court/litigation-related material) — the weaker of Oregon's two document types, since these aren't state-authored | `or/README.md` |
| Oregon | 3 Attorney General legal pleadings (`PLD-*.pdf`), state-employee-authored | Public agency record (state-authored) + fair use (litigation filings) — the stronger of Oregon's two document types | `or/README.md` |
| Colorado | Federal court Special Master reports (via RECAP) | Fair use (court filings) — not a public-domain claim | `co/README.md` |
| Colorado | State legislative (JBC) budget memos | Explicit written permission from JBC staff (2026-09-10) — the strongest footing of the seven document types in this table, not an inference from fair-use doctrine | `co/README.md` |
| Texas | State agency (HHSC) reports | HHSC's own conditional license (non-commercial, unaltered, attributed) — NOT public domain | `tx/README.md` |
| California | State agency (DSH) budget documents | Public domain — per case law and the absence of any restriction on the documents themselves, not a confirmed DSH policy adoption | `ca/README.md` |

**In short: California is public domain; Texas is conditionally licensed by its own publisher's
stated terms; Colorado's JBC budget memos have explicit written permission from JBC staff, the
strongest basis of any document type here; Washington's DSHS reports and Oregon's 3 AG-filed
pleadings rest on fair use with a public-agency-record backdrop (no copyright notice found, agency
itself openly publishes them, but no affirmative public-domain policy either); and Oregon's 15
court-monitor/neutral-expert reports and Colorado's Special Master reports rest on fair use alone,
backed by the same basis CourtListener/RECAP/Justia already rely on to redistribute filings at
scale (these are privately- or court-authored, not state agency work product, so they don't get
the agency-record backdrop the other documents have).** None of this is a formal legal opinion —
it's a documented, good-faith, per-document determination, exactly like the pre-publication safety
review below.

